1 NAME Shaul Brody /Katzenellenbogen/
2 GIVN Shaul Brody
2 SURN Katzenellenbogen
2 _MARNM Katzenellenbogen
2 NICK The Younger Rabbi of Chelm
2 NICK OF PINCZOW; OF CHELM
1 NAME Rabbi Saul Katzenellenbogen
1 NAME שאול //
2 GIVN שאול
2 SURN קצנלנבוגן
1 SEX M
1 OCCU unknown
2 PLAC Rabbi of Pinchov (S: D.Q, p.99)
2 ADDR
1 OCCU Rabbi
2 PLAC Chelm; Brody; Pinczów
2 ADDR
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1617
2 ADDR
3 CITY Chelm
3 STAE Lublin Voivodeship
3 CTRY Poland
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1691
2 ADDR
3 CITY Brody
3 STAE Lviv Oblast
3 CTRY Ukraine
1 OCCU Rabbi of Pinchov (S: D.Q, p.99), Rabbi, av beis din chelm
1 TITL Rabbi
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Saul Katzenellenbogen was born in 1617. He died in 1691.
2 CONT
2 CONT His first rabbinical position was A.B.D. of Brody, and later succeeded his father at Chem in about 1672.
2 CONT
2 CONT His last post was A.B.D. of Pinczow. He was named after his grandfather who died in the year he was born.  He wrote approbations to:
2 CONT # ''Lev Aryeh'' by Judah, son of Joshua, written at Brody in 1671, published Wilhermsdorf 1674.
2 CONT # ''Leket Shmuel'' by Samuel Feiwish, son of Joseph Katz, written in 1673 at Chelm, published Venice 1694.
2 CONT # ''Mayim Chayim'' by Haim, son of Joshua Horowitz, written in 1687 when he attended the Ciuncil meeting as Jaroslaw, published Dyhenfurth, 1690.
2 CONT # ''Beit Hillel'', by Hillel, son of Naftali Hirsch, published Dyhernfurth, 1691, where he signed his name "Saul K. of Cracow"
2 CONT # ''Tzintzenet Menachem'' by Menachem Mendel, son of Zvi Hirsch, written in 1689, published Berlin 1719/20.
2 CONT # ''Shabta DeRigla'' by Zvi Hirsch, son of Jerachmiel Catch, written in 1690 at Pinezow, published Furth 1693.  He died before its publication, as is mentioned, "Z.L." - of Blessed Memory, ie. 1691/2.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is buried next to Aaron Teomim who died two years earlier.
2 CONT
2 CONT he is also approved, as one of the Rabbis of the Council of the Four Lands, the publication of the German Bible and Aderet Eliyahu by Elijah Kalonymus of Lublin, published Frankfurt -on-Oder, 169, written in 1677 and 1678 respecti
2 CONC vely.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was married twice and had children with both wives.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Jewish Museum Jerusalem displays the Chevra Kadisha silver parcel-gilt cup from Schwabach with an inscription which reads:
2 CONT
2 CONT ''The Rabbi and A.B.D.of the Ansbach districts, Moses, son of the sainted Rabbi Saul, of Blessed Memory of the Katzenellenbogen family and his son Rabbi Phineas... there never arose a man like unto Moses.''
2 CONT
2 CONT Children from his first wife:
2 CONT * Moses, died young (G7.1 page 25)
2 CONT * Saadiah Isaiah (G7.2 page 25)
2 CONT
2 CONT Children from his second wife:
2 CONT * Jacob (G7.3 page 25)
2 CONT * Moses (G7.4 page 26)
2 CONT * Ephraim (G7.5 page 27)
2 CONT * Hinde (G7.6 page 27 - possibly from first wife)
2 CONT
2 CONT =Source=
2 CONT
2 CONT The Unbroken Chain by Neil Rosenstein (1990) Volume 1, Chapter 11, pages 24 and 25, G 6.6
