1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ([https://www.encyclopedia.com/religion/encyclopedias-almanacs-transcripts-and-maps/aryeh-judah-leib-ben-ephraim-ha-kohen on Encyclopedia.com]):ARYEH JUDAH LEIB BEN EPHRAIM HA-KOHEN (1658–1720), Moravian rabbi. Ary
2 CONC eh was the younger son of Ephraim b. Jacob ha-Kohen, rabbi of Ofen. He studied under his father together with his nephew ?evi Hirsch *Ashkenazi (the ?akham ?evi). His elder brother Hezekiah died in the plague which broke out in Of
2 CONC en in 1678 and Aryeh was taken ill. According to his own statement (introduction to the responsa Sha'ar Efrayim) his father prayed that he be taken instead of his son. Aryeh Leib recovered; but his father succumbed to the plague. 
2 CONC Before he died, he ordered his son to publish his books. After the death of his mother in 1684, Aryeh Judah decided to immigrate to Ere? Israel with his family and they were joined by Aaron, son of his deceased brother Hezekiah. T
2 CONC hey arrived in Jerusalem in 1685. There he began to arrange his father's book for publication although he found the preparatory work difficult; "it involved much trouble because of the confusing handwriting and the loss of many pa
2 CONC ges." About a year later he returned to Prague and published it under the title Sha'ar Efrayim (Sulzbach, 1688). It comprises 150 responsa on the four parts of the Shul?an Arukh. The end of the work contains Kunteres A?aron ("Last
2 CONC  Pamphlet") consisting of Aryeh's explanatory notes on the Talmud and the Tur, ?oshen Mishpat. Aryeh later returned to Ere? Israel, where he died in Safed. His son Jedidiah, pupil and later son-in-law of Abraham Yi??aki (the Sepha
2 CONC rdi rabbi of Jerusalem), also wrote responsa, only one of which was published.
2 CONT
2 CONT bibliography:
2 CONT Michael, Or, 364, 510, 524, 946; S.J. Fuenn, Kiryah Ne'emanah (1860), 73–74; J. Emden, Megillat Sefer, ed. by D. Kahana (1896), 4 ff.; D. Kaufmann, in: rej, 21 (1890), 135; idem, Die Erstuermung Ofens (1895), 18–19, 22, 24, 26–27;
2 CONC  idem, Gesammelte Schriften, 2 (1910), 303–5, 311–2; Frumkin-Rivlin, 2 (1928), 82–85.
