1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * [https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Nikolsburg_Hasidism '''Nikolsburg Hasidic dynasty] Wikipedia 
2 CONT * [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shmelke_of_Nikolsburg '''Shmelke of Nikolsburg'''] Wikipedia
2 CONT * [https://jewishencyclopedia.com/articles/7881-horowitz-schmelke '''Schmelke Horowitz] Jewish Encyclopedia
2 CONT * [https://www.encyclopedia.com/religion/encyclopedias-almanacs-transcripts-and-maps/horowitz-samuel-shmelke-nikolsburg '''Samuel Shmelke Horowitz Of Nikolsburg] Encyclopaedia Judaica
2 CONT * [https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=rYGwx55B_CU '''Paths of Faith: Descendants of Rabbi Shmelke of Nikolsburg - Hasidic Czech Documentary with English subtitles] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Rabbi and cabalist; born in Poland 1726; died at Nikolsburg April 28, 1778; son of R. Zvi Hirsch Horowitz, rabbi of Czortkow, and brother of R. Phinehas Horowitz of Frankfort-on-the-Main. A disciple of Dov Baer of Meseritz, he was
2 CONC  a devotee of the Kabala; and this brought him the reputation of a saint, to which he owed his call to Nikolsburg in 1773, after he had been rabbi of Ryczywol in Poland. In 1775 he was appointed chief rabbi of the province of Mora
2 CONC via. Horowitz's fame as a saint increased; and his arrival was supposed to have broken a long spell of drought. In Nikolsburg he established for those observing the Ḥasidic rite a synagogue ("Chasidimschul") which existed to the e
2 CONC nd of the nineteenth century. His cabalistic homilies on the Pentateuch were published under the title "Dibre Shemu'el" (Lemberg, c. 1870).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT === GEDCOM Note ===
2 CONT <p>Our most famous ancestor: He was a member of one of the famous and well documented Horowitz (Levite) rabbinic families in Eastern Europe..</p>tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horowitz Horowitz (Hebrew: הוֹרוֹביץ&lrm;, Yiddish: האָרא
2 CONC ָװיץ&lrm;, YIVO Horovits) is a surname that has its origin in the Yiddish name for the town of Hořovice (German: Horschowitz or Horowitz) in Bohemia. The patriarch of the family line is thought to be Aaron Meshullam Horowitz, foun
2 CONC der of Pinkas Synagogue in Prague, who lived in Hořovice and Prague in the 16th century, and had eight sons who spread the family throughout Europe, which later spread to the Middle East, the Russian Empire and the Americas. Today
2 CONC  there are some 50,000 people around the world &ndash; mostly of Jewish and more specifically Levite ancestry &ndash; carrying a variation of the Horowitz surname.[1] The Horowitz family is one of the most illustrious rabbinic fam
2 CONC iliesin Jewish history. Tradition quoted by scholars traces this family to the "sons of Korah" mentioned in the Bible: Numbers 26:11 and Psalms 47.[2] A family tree exists which traces Horowitz origins back to the 12th century.[3]
2 CONC  This family produced some of the greatest rabbinic scholars of Hachmei Provence, France, and Italy in the 12th, 13th, and 14th centuries.[4] The Sephardic surname of this family was Benveniste,[5] which was later changed to Horow
2 CONC itz upon their immigration to the town of Horowitz (near Prague)[6] in Bohemia in the 16th century.[7] From that time forward, prominent rabbis of this family were found in virtually every European country.[citation needed] In 201
2 CONC 6 historian and genealogist Edward Gelles established through Autosomal DNA the traditional belief concerning the descent of the Levitic Horowitz lines from the Halevi and Benveniste of Spain.[8] In 2017 Nature (Journal) Scientifi
2 CONC c Reports confirmed the allocation of Horowitz Levite samples was consistent with their proposed lineage and their genealogical claim could not be refuted.[9] Other variants of the name include Harowitz, Harrwitz, Harwitz, Horovit
2 CONC z, Horwicz, Horwitz, Hourwitz, Hurewicz, Hurwicz, Hurwitz, Gerovich, Gurevich, Gurvich, Gourevitch, Orowitz and Urwitz.[10] See also a short mention of his Biography, Students, Dinasty Works and References on Wikipedia::https://en
2 CONC .wikipedia.org/wiki/Shmelke_of_Nikolsburg He was named Shmuel ben Tzvi Hersh HaLevi (Horowitz) (commonly known by the diminutive "Shmelke") and was the oldest son of Tzvi Hirsh Horowitz of Chortkiv. A Levite, Shmuel Shmelke traced
2 CONC  his lineage back directly to the prophet Samuel who was also a Levite. In their early years, he and his brother Pinchas - who would also become a famous rabbi - studied Torah together.[3] Aftertraveling to Mezritch and meeting th
2 CONC e great hasidic master Dovber of Mezeritch, they became his ardent followers. After serving as rabbi in Ryczyw&oacute;ł and Shineva, Horowitz was invited, in 1773, to become the rabbi of Nikolsburg in Moravia, where he served unti
2 CONC l 1778.[4] On the first day of his being rabbi of Nikolsburg, Abraham Trebitsch, a native of Nikolsburg, reported him performing a miracle and bringing rain. It is told that his strong attachment to hassidism angered many members 
2 CONC of the community, which led to bitter quarrels that were quelled as a result of the personal intervention of Elimelech of Lizhensk. .. Rabbi Reb Shmelke Horowitz was (together with his brother Rabbi Pihas Horowitz (author of "HaFl
2 CONC ah") a disciple of Rabbi Dov Beer - the Maggid of Mezritch and the senior propagator of Hasidism. He and the Flah studied in non-Hasidic "lihuanian" yeshivot but after meeting the Maggid, became his ardent followers. He served fir
2 CONC st in Richwal and Shiniava and was electedin 1773 as Chief Rabbi of Nikolsburg in Moravia (today Mikulov in the Check Republic) where he created a major Yeshiva that attracted outstsanding students -themselves future senior hasidi
2 CONC c masters (see short list in Notes, Citation Note 1 page 29). Most books on Hasidism mention him and his teachings (See a short bibliography in Notes, Citation Notes).</p>
2 CONT Source Link: https://www.geni.com/documents/view?doc_id=6000000183443733824label=@S2@
