2 CONT *Rosen, Rabbi Michael (2008). [http://www.urimpublications.com/quest-for-authenticity-the-thought-of-reb-simhah-bunim.html The Quest for Authenticity: the thought of Reb Simhah Bunim]. Urim Publications. ISBN 9789655240030. OCLC 1
2 CONC 90789076.
2 CONT *Boim, Rabbi Yehudah Menahem (1977). [https://mysefer.com/Product.aspx?ProductID=781&strReferer=search&PPGR=0 Ha-Rabi Rabi Bunem mi-Pshisḥah] (in Hebrew) 
2 CONT *Brill, Alan (1997). [https://www.academia.edu/22077752/GRANDEUR_AND_HUMILITY_IN_THE_WRITINGS_OF_R_SIMHAH_BUNIM_OF_PRZYSUCHA Grandeur and humility in the writings of R. Simhah Bunim of Przysucha]. Michael Sharf Publication 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Rabbi Simcha Bunim Bonhardt of Peshischa''' (ca. 1765 – September 4, 1827) also known as the '''Rebbe Reb Bunim''' was the second Grand Rabbi of Peshischa (Przysucha, Poland) as well as one of the key leaders of Hasidic Judaism
2 CONC  in Poland. The main disciple of R. Yaakov Yitzchak Rabinowicz ("the Yid Ha-Kadosh"), from 1813 to 1827, he led the Peshischa movement of Hasidic thought, in which he revolutionized 19th-century Hasidic philosophy by juxtaposing t
2 CONC he rationalistic pietism of German-Jewry with the spiritual nature of God defined by the Hasidic movement. He was instrumental in challenging the Hasidic status quo, in which he paired enlightenment philosophy with traditional Ort
2 CONC hodox Judaism while controversially emphasizing the importance of the individual in regards to one's personal relationship with God. He outwardly challenged the dynastic and autocratic nature of Hasidic rebbes and encouraged the d
2 CONC emocratization of Judaism, which led to several unsuccessful attempts by contemporary Hasidic leadership to excommunicate him. Above all else, he believed that authenticity and self-honesty were the foundation of true piety and th
2 CONC at the pursuance of authenticity should always usurp the status quo. His teachings are foundational for Kotzk Hasidism, Ger Hasidism, Amshinov Hasidism, Zychlin Hasidism, Aleksander Hasidism, Vurka Hasidism, Sochatchov Hasidim, Ra
2 CONC dzymin Hasidism, Lublin Hasidism, Strikov Hasidism, Lelov Hasidism, Kuzmir Hasidism and Izhbitza-Radzin Hasidism. Because of his widespread influence on Polish Hasidism, many consider R. Simcha Bunim to be one of the most importan
2 CONC t Jewish philosophers of the Napoleonic era.
