1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Avraham Yehoshua Heshel, Apter Rebbe / Rov - רבי אברהם יהושע העשיל  ( Born: 1748, Nowy Żmigród - Died	5 Nisan 1825, Medzhybizh )   Main work	''אוהב ישראל Oheiv Yisrael'' - הרב הגאון הצדיק אברהם יהשוע השיל אדמו"ר מא
2 CONC פטא 
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Oheiv Yisrael'' was published in Zhitomir in 1863 and he was also the author of ''Torat Emet'', published in Lvov in 1854.
2 CONT
2 CONT A scion of famous rabbinic families, on both his father's and his mother's side (his family can be traced back to Maharam Padua and Saul Wahl), Avraham Yehoshua Heshel showed great promise even at an early age.
2 CONT
2 CONT Acquiring fame as a talmudic scholar, he studied under Rabbi Elimelech of Lizhensk and Rabbi Yechiel Michl of Zlotchov. Becoming one of the foremost spokesmen of the growing Chasidic movement in Poland, he began his career as Rabb
2 CONC i of Kolbasov.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1800 he accepted the post of Rabbi of Apt, or Opatów. Although he held many other rabbinic positions, to the chasidim he remained always the Apter Rov.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1808 he was chosen as Rabbi of Iaşi, Moldavia. In the wake of communal strife there he was forced to leave his post and settled in Mezhbizh, the home of the Baal Shem Tov and the cradle of Hasidism, where he devoted himself com
2 CONC pletely to the study and dissemination of Chasidism.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was during this period in his life, in Mezhbizh, that he gained the veneration of thousands of followers, among whom were a number of the prominent rabbis of the age. His outstanding character trait was his strongly expressed l
2 CONC ove of the Jewish people, which earned him the title of Oheiv Yisrael, "Lover of Israel". Oheiv Yisrael became the title of the published collection of his thoughts arranged according to the weekly Torah portions. The work abounds
2 CONC  with lofty kabbalistic insights and interpretations. It is one of the basic chasidic texts, and bespeaks the Apter Rov's passionate love of his fellow Jews. The Apter Rov is one of the most notable and beloved luminaries on the h
2 CONC asidic firmament.
2 CONT
2 CONT On his deathbed, crying bitterly over the long exile, he said: "Before his demise Rabbi Levi Yitzchak of Berdichev promised that upon entering the World-to-Come he would not rest or sit until Mashiach would come. But they diverted
2 CONC  his attention by teaching him lofty and mystical concepts, until he forgot his pledge—but I assure you, I will not forget."
2 CONT
2 CONT He was buried in Mezhbizh, near the Baal Shem Tov. An ornate stone ohel marks his grave in the old Jewish cemetery. According to one hasidic legend, angels subsequently carried his body and buried him in the Holy Land, and in the 
2 CONC Jewish Cemetery in Tiberias there is a stone marking his supposed grave.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Hasidic dynasty of Mezhbizh/Zinkov
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT Chapin, David A. and Weinstock, Ben, The Road from Letichev: The history and culture of a forgotten Jewish community in Eastern Europe, Volume 1. ISBN 0-595-00666-3 iUniverse, Lincoln, NE, 2000.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kaplan, Aryeh (1991) Chasidic Masters: History, Biography, Thought. Moznaim Publishing Corporation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rabinowicz, Tzvi M. The Encyclopedia of Hasidism: ISBN 1-56821-123-6 Jason Aronson, Inc., 1996.
2 CONT
2 CONT Finkel, Avraham Y. The Great Chasidic Masters: ISBN 1-56821-939-3 Jason Aronson, Inc., 1992.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://wapedia.mobi/en/Apta_%28Hasidic_dynasty%29
2 CONT
2 CONT Rabbi Avraham Yehoshua Heshel of Apt was the founder of the Apt-Mezhbizh-Zinkover Hasidic dynasty. In honor of the dynasty's founder, his descendants adopted the family name Heshel.
2 CONT
2 CONT The males in this family took wives several different times from the family of the Ryzhiner Rebbe.
2 CONT
2 CONT     * R. Avraham Yehoshua Heshel of Apt (1748-1825)
2 CONT
2 CONT         * R. Yitschak Meir of Zinkov (1776-1854)
2 CONT
2 CONT         * R. Yosef Moshe of Mezhbizh
2 CONT
2 CONT             * R. Meshulam Zusia of Zinkov (1813-1865)
2 CONT
2 CONT                 * R. Chaim Menachem of Zinkov (1837-1894)
2 CONT
2 CONT                     * R. Pinchas of Zinkov (1872-1916)
2 CONT
2 CONT                         * R. Abraham Joshua Heshel of Zinkov
2 CONT
2 CONT                     * R. Moses of Zinkov (1879-1923)
2 CONT
2 CONT                         * R. Chaim Menachem of Zinkov-Bnei Brak
2 CONT
2 CONT                 * R. Abraham Joshua Heshel of Mezhbizh (1832-1880)
2 CONT
2 CONT                     * R. Israel Shalom Joseph of Mezhbizh (1853-1911)
2 CONT
2 CONT                         * R. Yitshak Meir of Mezhbizh-Haifa (1904-1985)
2 CONT
2 CONT                         * R. Abraham Joshua Heshel of Mezhbizh-Tarnopol (1892-1943)
2 CONT
2 CONT                     * R. Yitshak Meir of Kopyczynce (1862-1936)
2 CONT
2 CONT                         * R. Abraham Joshua Heshel of Kopyczynce (1888-1967). His daughter Chava was married to Shneur Zalman Gurary, a chassid of the last two Lubavitcher Rebbes [1] .
2 CONT
2 CONT                             * R. Moshe Mordechai Heshel of Kopyczynce (1914-1975) Died suddenly of a cerebral hemorrhage.
2 CONT
2 CONT                                 * R. Avraham Y. Heschel, the youngest son of R. Moshe Mordechai Heshel ZT"L. In 1999, together with the assistance of his mother and older brother R. Isaac M. Heschel, they founded Chasdei Moshe-Kop
2 CONC yczynitz an outreach organization in Brooklyn, N.Y. dedicated in memory of the late Grand Rabbi and the continuity of the Kopyczynitzers. A division of that organization was inaugurated in the fall of 2000, the Brandler Institute 
2 CONC of Chasidic Thought, a think tank devoted to bringing a greater understanding of Chasidic culture to the secular society.
2 CONT
2 CONT                             * R. Meshulam Zusia Heschel, the youngest son of R. Abraham Joshua Heschel
2 CONT
2 CONT                                 * Rabbi Yitzchak Meir Flitenstein, grandson of Reb Abraham Joshua Heshel is currently the Kapishnitzer Rebbe in Jerusalem. He is the founder and director of the Ohev Yisrael Institutions, the world 
2 CONC famous Machon Sifsei Tzaddikim for publishing of manuscripts and chassidic writings. He has a large following in both Israel and around the world.
2 CONT
2 CONT                     * R. Meshulam Zusia of Mezhbizh (1871-1920)
2 CONT
2 CONT                     * R. Moses Mordecai of Mezhbizh (1873-1917)
2 CONT
2 CONT                         * Prof. R. Abraham Joshua Heschel (January 11, 1907, Warsaw, Poland - December 23, 1972), Professor of Jewish Ethics and Mysticism at Jewish Theological Seminary of America (JTS), the main seminary of Conse
2 CONC rvative Judaism. He was an important author, civil rights activist, and leader in the Conservative movement.
2 CONT
2 CONT                             * Prof. Susannah Heschel (1952- ), Professor of Jewish Studies at Dartmouth and an important modern Jewish scholar.
2 CONT
