1 NAME Abraham Abusch /of Lissa/ ABD Frankfurt-on-Main
2 GIVN Abraham Abusch
2 NSFX ABD Frankfurt-on-Main
2 _MARNM of Lissa
2 NICK Birkat Avraham
1 NAME הגאון המפורסם מו"ה רבי אברהם אבוש מליסא(אב"ד פרנקפורט,מח"ס ברכת אברהם)
1 NAME אברהם /אבוש מליסא/ אב"ד פרנקפורט
2 GIVN אברהם
2 SURN אבוש מליסא
2 NSFX אב"ד פרנקפורט
1 NAME הגאון המפורסם מו"ה רבי אברהם אבוש מליסא(אב"ד פרנקפורט,מח"ס ברכת אברהם)
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1690
1 DEAT
2 DATE 22 SEP 1768
1 TITL Rabbi
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Abraham Abusch, a very modest man, was the Rabbi  in Janow, Lukow, Mezerich, Lissa, and finally in Frankfurt am Main 1759 - 1768 (Tishri 11).  Rabbi Abraham's father, Zvi Hirsch was also ABD in Mezerich.  They are 
2 CONC both buried in Frankfurt am Main.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abraham's son, Hirsch Lissa was born in Frankfurt.  He died in 1777 and was buried beneath his Father.  This was told to Dorothy Lowenstein by Michael Lenarz when she was at the museum in Frankfurt in the 1980s.
2 CONT
2 CONT Rabbi Abraham was the author of [http://hebrewbooks.org/33496 Kaneh Avraham קנה אברהם] which is "Birkhat Avraham ברכת אברהם". It is a kabbalistic commentary, in the manner of Pardes, on Genesis. It has a number of approbations fol
2 CONC lowed by the introduction and the text. R. Abraham Abusch ben Zevi Hirsch (1700–1769) was a German rabbi and halakhist. He was also called R. Abraham Abusch Lissa and also R. Abusch Frankfurter, from the towns Lissa and Frankfurt 
2 CONC where he served as rabbi, after having been rabbi of Mezhirech. After the interregnum brought about by the departure of R. Jacob Joshua Falk, the community of Frankfurt approached him to become its rabbi. The community of Lissa wa
2 CONC s reluctant to part with him and only did so after much persuasion on the part of the communal leaders of Frankfurt. His pious and meek disposition and the stories of his charitable deeds became legendary. The name of R. Abraham A
2 CONC busch is associated with a cause célèbre, "the Cleves get". Although several renowned rabbis approved the divorce, Abraham persisted in his opinion that it was invalid. The members of his community supported him by enacting a regu
2 CONC lation barring from the Frankfurt rabbinate anyone who had approved the divorce. For some time, he also held the important position of parnas or president of the "*Councils of the Lands ." Although he was renowned as a talmudic sc
2 CONC holar, few of his writings have survived. Several of his works appeared under the title Birkat Avraham: (1) novellae on five tractates of Seder Mo'ed (1881); (2) commentary on the Passover Haggadah (1887), with a supplement, Mahaz
2 CONC eh Avraham (1908); (3) a volume also known as Kaneh Avraham (1884); (4) a commentary on Berakhot (1930); and (5) on Ruth (1934). He also wrote Darkhei ha-Hayyim, on remedies, medicines, and charms (1912). His ethical will was also
2 CONC  published (1806).  From: Hassidut, Kabbalah - Tiferet Uzziel; Kaneh Avraham   (תפארת עוזיאל : קנה אברהם) Author:    R. Uzziel ben Zevi Hirsch Meisels;R.Abraham Abusch City:   Bilgoraj; Warsaw Publication Date:  1909; 1908
2 CONT
2 CONT In the book "Memorable Dates of Jewish History" by Gotthard Deutsch, there is an entry giving the date of death of Abraham Lissa, Rabbi of Frankfurt, as 1769
2 CONT
2 CONT See "Sources" for a charming story about R. Abraham Abush.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following are variations of his name:
2 CONT
2 CONT Abraham Abusch Ben Zevi Hirsch  Abraham Abusch Lissa [VWLCAuth]  Abraham Abusch ben Ẓevi Hirsch [LCAuth]  Abraham Lissa [Enc. Judaica (dt. Ausgabe)]  Abraham ben Zevi Hirsch  Abraham ben Zwi Hirsch  Abraham ben Ẓebi Hirsch [Jewi
2 CONC sh Encyclopedia 1901ff Bd. 8, S. 107]  Abusch Frankfurter [VWLCAuth]  Avish, Avraham [VWLCAuth]  Avraham Abish [VWLCAuth]  Avraham Avish  Avrāhām Abîš ʼBʺD DQʺQ Lîssâ WQʺQ Frânqfûrṭ dĕ-Mayn [GBV]  Avrāhām Abûš Ben Ṣ
2 CONC ĕvî Hîrš mi-Franqfûrṭ dĕ-Mayn [GBV]  Frankfurter, Abusch [VWLCAuth]  Lissa, Abraham  Lissa, Abraham Abush 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Author of ''Birkat Avraham'', published in Warsaw in 1881 [on Tractate Mo'ed], in 1905 [on the Torah], and in 1934 [on the Book of Ruth].
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT https://he.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%A8%D7%94%D7%9D_%D7%90%D7%91%D7%99%D7%A9_%D7%9E%D7%9C%D7%99%D7%A1%D7%90
2 CONT
2 CONT שימש כאב בית דין במספר מקומות,האחרון בפרנפורט
