1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abraham_Naftali_Hertz_Scheuer Abraham Naftali Hertz Scheuer] Wikipedia 
2 CONT
2 CONT Abraham Naftali Hertz Scheuer was born in Frankfurt am Main in 1753 to his father Rabbi David Tebele Scheuer. He led his father's yeshiva in Mainz as its Rosh Yeshiva from 1778 until 1782, taking over from his brother Rabbi Mechel
2 CONC  Scheuer. In 1811 he became Rabbi of Mainz where he died in 1822. He is known for his halakhic ruling against the use of an organ in synagogues in the book Eleh Dibre ha-B'rit.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.ivelt.com/forum/viewtopic.php?t=14227
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abraham_Naftali_Hertz_Scheuer
2 CONT
2 CONT Abraham Naftali Hertz Scheuer was a German Orthodox rabbi and kabbalist, born in Frankfurt am Main in 1753 to Rabbi David Tebele Scheuer and his second wife (daughter of Rabbi Nathan Otiz, rabbi of Bamberg).
2 CONT
2 CONT He led his father's yeshiva in Mainz as its Rosh Yeshiva from 1778 until 1782, taking over from his brother Rabbi Mechel Scheuer. In 1811 he became Rabbi of Mainz where he died on October 10, 1822.[1] He is known for his halakhic 
2 CONC ruling against the use of an organ in synagogues in the book Eleh Dibre ha-B'rit (Altona 1819).
2 CONT
2 CONT Notable students
2 CONT Jakob Löwenstein
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT  See Moshe Samet, Ha-Shinuyim b'Sidrei Beit-ha-K'neset, Asufot 5, Jerusalem 1991, 345-404: p. 377.
